 questions: [
  ["Serial extraction is most appropriate for which of the following malocclusions?","Class I with severe crowding and a normal overbite",["Class II division 2 with crowding and a deep overbite","Class III with an anterior crossbite and mild crowding","Class I with generalised spacing and an open bite"],"Serial extraction suits crowding in an otherwise ideal Class I pattern. A deep bite worsens with it, and skeletal Class II or III problems need growth modification or later camouflage or surgery instead."],
  ["The most common order of serial extraction in the maxillary arch is:","Primary canines, then first primary molars, then first premolars",["First primary molars, then first premolars, then primary canines","First premolars, then primary canines, then first primary molars","Primary canines, then second primary molars, then second premolars"],"Dewel's C-D-4 sequence relieves incisor crowding first, then speeds first premolar eruption so the premolar can be removed before the canine erupts into its space."],
  ["In serial extraction the first primary molars are removed mainly to:","Speed eruption of the first premolars ahead of the canines",["Allow the first permanent molars to drift mesially early","Relieve crowding of the mandibular incisors directly now","Stop the second premolars from erupting too early"],"With D gone, the first premolar erupts early and can be extracted before the permanent canine arrives. Mesial drift of the first molar would use up the space the plan needs."],
  ["Serial extraction is more difficult to apply in the mandible than in the maxilla mainly because the mandibular:","Canine often erupts before the first premolar",["First premolar often erupts before the canine","Second premolar is often congenitally missing","Incisors erupt later than the first molars"],"In the mandible the usual sequence is 6-1-2-3-4-5-7, so the canine may arrive before the first premolar can be removed; the premolar sometimes has to be taken out early. In the maxilla the first premolar normally comes first, which suits the classic order."],
  ["A recognised side effect of serial extraction is:","Lingual tipping of the incisors with a deeper overbite",["Labial flaring of the incisors with an anterior open bite","Mesial rotation of the first permanent molars","Accelerated root resorption of the first molars"],"As the incisors drift back into the canine space they tip lingually and the bite deepens, which is why a deep bite is a contraindication. Labial flaring is the opposite of what happens."],
  ["A 9-year-old has 10 mm of crowding in each arch, a 7 mm overjet, a deep overbite and a retrusive mandible. Regarding serial extraction, the most appropriate statement is that it is:","Not indicated, because the skeletal pattern is Class II",["Indicated, because the crowding reaches 10 mm per arch","Indicated, starting with the first premolars","Not indicated until the second molars have erupted"],"Serial extraction is a Class I procedure. Here the overjet, deep bite and retrusive mandible need assessment for growth modification first, and extractions would worsen the deep bite."],
  ["A 25-year-old has a Class II division 1 malocclusion with a 7 mm overjet, an aligned lower arch and an acceptable profile. Growth has finished and she declines surgery. The most acceptable extraction pattern is removal of the:","Maxillary first premolars only",["Mandibular first premolars only","Maxillary and mandibular second premolars","Mandibular second premolars only"],"Removing the upper first premolars lets the upper incisors be retracted to reduce the overjet, leaving Class II molars with Class I canines. Removing lower premolars alone would let the lower incisors drop back and increase the overjet."],
  ["A 17-year-old with a skeletal Class III is in pre-surgical orthodontics. After 6 months his reverse overjet has increased from 2 mm to 6 mm. The most likely explanation is that:","The incisor compensations are being removed as planned",["Anchorage has been lost from the first permanent molars","The mandible has grown faster than expected","The archwires are delivering far too much force to the incisors"],"Decompensation uprights proclined upper and retroclined lower incisors over their bone, so the true jaw discrepancy shows. The surgeon then moves the jaws the full amount into a stable occlusion."],
  ["The surgical procedure most commonly used to set back or advance the mandible is the:","Bilateral sagittal split osteotomy",["Le Fort I osteotomy","Le Fort III osteotomy","Surgically assisted palatal expansion"],"The BSSO splits each ramus so the tooth-bearing mandible can slide forward or back. Le Fort I moves the maxilla, and Le Fort III is a craniofacial procedure for syndromic midface deficiency."],
  ["A 28-year-old has a bilateral posterior crossbite from a narrow maxilla and normal molar inclinations. The most appropriate way to widen the maxilla is:","Surgically assisted rapid palatal expansion",["Rapid maxillary expansion without surgery","A quad helix to tip the molars buccally","Cross elastics on the first molars"],"After adolescence the midpalatal suture interdigitates and fuses, so orthopaedic expansion alone tips teeth and fails. Surgery releases the resistance so the maxillary halves can separate."],
  ["In a child with a unilateral cleft lip and palate, the alveolar bone graft is usually placed:","At about 8 to 11 years, before the canine erupts",["At about 3 months, with the lip repair","At about 12 months, with the palate repair","At about 18 years, after facial growth has finished"],"Grafting in the mixed dentition gives the canine bone to erupt through and stabilises the maxillary segments; it is timed by canine root development rather than by the infant repairs."],
  ["The tooth most often missing or malformed in a child with a cleft of the alveolus is the:","Maxillary lateral incisor",["Maxillary central incisor","Maxillary canine","Maxillary first premolar"],"The cleft runs between the lateral incisor and canine regions, so the lateral incisor at the cleft is often missing, peg-shaped or duplicated."],
  ["Palate repair in a child with a cleft is usually completed by about 9 to 18 months mainly to:","Allow normal speech to develop",["Allow the alveolar bone graft to be placed","Prevent the primary molars from erupting","Improve forward growth of the maxilla"],"Early palate closure gives the child a sealed palate for speech. The repair scar actually tends to restrict maxillary growth, which is the trade-off."],
  ["A 15-year-old with a repaired unilateral cleft lip and palate has a reverse overjet and a concave profile. The most likely explanation is:","Restricted maxillary growth from repair scar tissue",["Excess mandibular growth caused directly by the cleft","Forward shift of the mandible on closure","Overeruption of the mandibular incisors"],"Scar from palate surgery limits forward and transverse growth of the maxilla, so cleft patients tend toward a skeletal Class III from maxillary deficiency, not from mandibular excess."]
 ]
